{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015sEU37jQgq3JeHPpSiGGHA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that re-implements Unix utilities in Go, using C source code as the specification source of truth and differential testing against GNU reference binaries for verification.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with:\n- `cmd/` - utility implementations\n- `pkg/sys/` - syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - output formatting\n- `pkg/testutils/` - differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - specifications\n\n**Current Implementation State**: The core infrastructure is in place with differential testing utilities, platform-specific syscall abstractions for Darwin and Linux, output formatting modules, and working implementations of cat and wc utilities with their test suites.\n\n**Releases and their status**: There's a discrepancy between the recorded status and actual source code—several packages marked as \"NOT started\" in the release tracking actually exist in the codebase, including the foundational utilities and multiple command implementations like sponge, du, ls, true, false, and version.\n\nI need to trust what the source code shows rather than the prose descriptions, so I'm reconciling the actual state of the codebase against the release status records.\n\nLooking at what's actually implemented in the source tree, I can see the packages and commands that exist with their test coverage. Now I need to figure out what's genuinely missing from the releases versus what's already been coded but just not properly documented. the releases in scope?\n\nrel00.0 (magefiles, format, sys, testutils):\n- magefiles is excluded from generation (DD7)\n- format, sys, testutils all exist in source_code\n- cmd/version exists\n- So rel00.0 is essentially done from code perspective\n\nrel01.1 (cat): cmd/cat exists with main.go and cat_test.go — DONE\n\nrel01.2 (sponge): cmd/sponge exists with main.go and sponge_test.go — DONE\n\nrel01.3 (du): cmd/du exists with main.go and du_test.go — DONE\n\nrel02.0 (true, false, yes, basename, dirname):\n- cmd/true and cmd/false exist but lack test files\n- cmd/yes, cmd/basename, and cmd/dirname don't exist yet\n- No PRDs available, so specs are still pending\n\nrel02.1 (tee) and rel02.2 (head, seq) are both spec-pending with no code started.\n\nrel03.0 (wc) and rel04.0 (ls) are complete with tests, while rel04.1 (ls-extended) has specs but no additional implementation beyond the existing ls command.\n\nThe first real gap is rel02.0, which needs test coverage for true and false plus full implementation of the three missing commands, but I'm blocked on specs. Looking at the constraint that I can only propose one task, the highest priority is clear: write differential tests for the `true` command since it already has both implementation and a PRD, just missing test coverage. The tests would need to verify the core requirements like exiting with status 0, producing no output, and silently ignoring non-flag arguments. Looking at the true utility's requirements, I'm seeing 8 sub-requirements total which exceeds the 4-requirement limit per task. I need to scope this down to a reasonable subset for testing—the cat tests cover a much broader range, so I should pick the most critical requirements for true and propose the rest as a separate task. I need to be more careful about what counts as a sub-requirement versus a requirement group. Looking at the actual structure, R1, R2, R3, R4 are the groups, and things like R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5 are the individual sub-requirements I can pick from. For the `true` command test task, I should select exactly 4 sub-requirements that cover the core behavior—exits with 0, no stdout output, no stderr output, and silently ignoring non-flag arguments.\n\nI realize I don't actually have access to the PRD for `true` (prd012-true), so I should make sure that gets included in the required reading for the stitch agent. I'm also wondering whether there's already an existing test suite I should be aware of before proposing a new test structure.\n\nLooking at the release information, rel02.0 shows a \"pending\" spec status, but the implementation is already there. That means there might or might not be formal test documentation. Given that I can only propose one task, the most valuable thing would be to write differential tests for the `true` command. Let me verify that `true` and `false` are actually part of rel02.0 by checking the code status—yes, they're listed as rel02.0-uc001 and rel02.0-uc002 respectively, along with other utilities like `yes`, `basename`, and `dirname`.\n\nSince I'm working with alphabetical ordering, I should tackle `cmd/true` tests before `cmd/false`, and both before the others that still need implementation. Looking at what the `true` command actually does based on the source code, it needs to handle exiting with status 0 and producing no output, silently ignoring non-flag arguments, responding to `--help` and `--version` flags appropriately, and handling SIGPIPE correctly.\n\nFor the differential tests, I'm focusing on the core behavior: verifying exit code 0, no stdout, no stderr, and that non-flag arguments are silently ignored. The reference binary `gtrue` provides a simple baseline—it just exits cleanly with no output—so the tests should validate that our implementation matches that behavior.\n\nI'm hitting a constraint tension here: the task limits me to 4 sub-requirements maximum, but a test file covering just those 4 would land around 80-120 lines, which falls short of the 150-250 line target. Since I can't add more requirements, I'll stick with the 4 core ones and accept that this particular task will be smaller.\n\nActually, let me step back and look at what else needs work across all the releases. The `true` command tests are straightforward, but maybe there's a more substantial task I should tackle instead. Looking at the full scope, I see several releases with incomplete work—some have code but no tests, others have neither code nor specs yet. Let me map out what's actually missing to find a better-scoped task.\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is rel02.0, which has `true` and `false` with code but no tests, while `yes`, `basename`, and `dirname` don't exist yet. For the ordering, I need to check whether the use case IDs or alphabetical order takes precedence when deciding what to implement first.\n\nLooking at the requirements, both `true` and `false` share most of the same patterns—exit codes, help/version flags, SIGPIPE handling, and argument handling—so I could potentially write tests that cover both commands together rather than separately.\n\nSince alphabetical ordering puts `cmd/false` first and I'm limited to one task, I'll focus on differential tests for the `false` command, targeting the four most critical requirements: the exit code behavior, help and version output, SIGPIPE handling, and argument ignoring. Looking at the source code, I can see that R1.1, R1.2, and R1.3 all describe the same behavior—exiting with code 1 and producing no output. R1.5 and R4.1 are about silently ignoring non-flag arguments while maintaining that exit code 1 behavior.\n\nTo create more meaningful test scenarios, I should focus on the cases where the exit code actually changes: --help and --version both override the default exit code to 0. So I'll structure the requirements around the core exit behavior, argument handling, and the flag-specific overrides that produce different outcomes. I'm realizing that comparing --help and --version output between our implementation and GNU false would require normalizing the text since they'll have different formatting and version strings. For practical testing, I should focus on the core behaviors: exit codes with no arguments, with random arguments, and the exit code changes for --help and --version, while skipping the actual output comparison for those flags since they'll naturally differ. I'm going to focus on the core behavior tests that work well with differential testing—R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R1.5—since those test the fundamental exit code behavior without running into output format differences that would require awkward normalizers. Task: Differential tests for cmd/false (prd013-false R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5)\n\nThe task creates `cmd/false/false_test.go` with differential tests covering:\n- R1.1: exit code 1 unconditionally\n- R1.2: no output to stdout\n- R1.3: no output to stderr  \n- R1.5: non-flag arguments silently ignored (exit still 1)\n\nRequired reading:\n- docs/specs/product-requirements/prd013-false.yaml (the PRD)\n- cmd/false/main.go (the implementation to test)\n- pkg/testutils/difftest.go (the testing harness)\n- cmd/true/main.go (reference for similar patterns)\n- An existing test file like cmd/cat/cat_test.go or cmd/wc/wc_test.go for pattern reference\n\nI'll follow the TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go, building the Go binary and locating the reference `gfalse` binary via exec.LookPath, skipping if unavailable. Using testutils.RunDiffTests for all test cases with `gfalse` as the reference binary. The tests need to cover exit code 1 with no args, no stdout/stderr output, and proper handling of non-flag arguments while maintaining exit code 1. So the test cases are straightforward—just verify that `false` exits with code 1 and produces no output regardless of whether it's run with or without arguments. Now I'm thinking through the test file structure, making sure to follow the existing patterns with `t.Parallel()` and the proper copyright header that all the other test files use. Let me write out the final task YAML with all the requirements captured. Looking at the specific test requirements now: I need to verify that false exits with code 1, produces no output to stdout or stderr when run with no arguments, and silently ignores non-flag arguments while still exiting with code 1. I'm planning to follow the same TestMain pattern used in the cat tests, building the Go binary and locating the GNU reference implementation to run differential comparisons. I'm realizing that in a differential test setup, the \"no stdout\" and \"no stderr\" requirements are automatically verified when comparing outputs between the two binaries — if both produce nothing, the test passes. So those requirements are covered by the same test case that checks the exit code, which is fine since they're distinct requirements being validated together.\n\nLooking at how the existing tests are structured, they use a single test function with multiple DiffTest entries that all run through the same comparison framework, so I should follow that pattern with test names like TestFalseNoArgs and TestFalseIgnoresArgs. I'm estimating the test file would land around 80-100 lines total—well within the 250-line constraint—so the structure feels right.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":91374,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":91374},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4bcb3daa-2683-4961-9cf1-6a4f49c2d1bf","uuid":"409d50b5-660b-4d87-bb9c-48a04419db67"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015sEU37jQgq3JeHPpSiGGHA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils re-implements Unix utilities in Go from C-source-derived specifications, using differential testing against GNU reference binaries to verify behavioral parity. It's an experiment in specification-driven code generation via the mage-claude-orchestrator.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (one package per utility), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstraction), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, excluded from generation).\n\n**Implementation State** (from source code, trusted over prose):\n- **Done**: pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/cat (+tests), cmd/wc (+tests), cmd/sponge (+tests), cmd/du (+tests), cmd/ls (+tests), cmd/version, cmd/true (no tests), cmd/false (no tests)\n- **Missing**: cmd/yes, cmd/basename, cmd/dirname, cmd/tee, cmd/head, cmd/seq, ls-extended features\n\n**Current Release**: rel02.0 (trivial utilities: true, false, yes, basename, dirname). Within this release, `cmd/true` and `cmd/false` have implementations but no test files. `yes`, `basename`, and `dirname` have no code and specs are pending. The orphaned PRDs prd012-true and prd013-false confirm specs exist for those two utilities.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nEarliest incomplete release is rel02.0. Within it, `cmd/false` and `cmd/true` have implementations but lack differential tests. Per canonical ordering (P8): tests come after implementation, and `cmd/false` sorts before `cmd/true` alphabetically. The false test file is the highest-priority deliverable.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Differential tests for cmd/false -- prd013-false R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd013-false.yaml (PRD for false utility -- requirements and acceptance criteria)\n      - cmd/false/main.go (implementation under test -- exit behavior, --help, --version, SIGPIPE)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (differential testing harness API -- DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests, NormalizeFunc)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for TestMain build/locate, normalizeProgramName, and RunDiffTests usage)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/false/false_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for false core exit behavior and argument ignoring\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test prd013-false R1.1: false exits with code 1 when invoked with no arguments. Verify via differential test that both Go binary and gfalse reference binary produce exit code 1.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test prd013-false R1.2 and R1.3: false produces no output to stdout and no output to stderr when invoked with no arguments. Verified implicitly by RunDiffTests byte-for-byte stdout and stderr comparison -- both binaries must produce empty output.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test prd013-false R1.5: false silently ignores non-flag arguments and still exits with code 1. Pass arbitrary arguments (e.g., 'foo', 'bar', '--unknown') and verify exit code 1 with no output.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement TestMain following the project pattern: build the Go false binary into a temp directory, locate the gfalse reference binary via exec.LookPath, skip all tests gracefully (os.Exit(0)) if gfalse is not found on PATH.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go: declare package-level goBinary and refBinary variables, build Go binary with exec.Command('go', 'build', '-o', goBinary, '.'), locate reference binary with exec.LookPath('gfalse'), skip with os.Exit(0) if not found, clean up binDir after m.Run().\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use testutils.RunDiffTests for all test cases. Each DiffTest specifies Args, ExitCode, and optionally Stdin. RunDiffTests compares stdout, stderr, and exit code byte-for-byte between Go and reference binaries.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"The reference binary name is 'gfalse', following the Homebrew GNU coreutils g-prefix convention used by other utilities in this project (gcat, gls, gdu, gwc).\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"All test functions call t.Parallel() at the top, matching the convention in all existing test files (cmd/cat/cat_test.go, cmd/wc/wc_test.go, cmd/du/du_test.go).\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/false/false_test.go compiles without errors (go vet ./cmd/false/).\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"All differential tests pass when gfalse is on PATH (go test ./cmd/false/).\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests skip gracefully with os.Exit(0) in TestMain and a message to stderr when gfalse is not found on PATH.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Test coverage includes: (1) no-arguments exits 1 with no output, (2) single non-flag argument ignored exits 1, (3) multiple arguments ignored exits 1.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"File includes copyright header 'Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.' and SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT, matching all existing source files.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":91374,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":91374},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4bcb3daa-2683-4961-9cf1-6a4f49c2d1bf","uuid":"6d87bc7f-b046-4842-97cd-6156d3d081de"}
